That is a great use case. In genealogy charts, visual distinction is
critical, and SVG's `stroke-dasharray` combined with line properties gives
you complete freedom to map every relationship type to a distinct style.

Here are standard pattern strategies you can use in SVG to represent
different relationship tiers:

### Relationship Style Mapping

| Relationship Type | Visual Style | SVG Implementation |
| --- | --- | --- |
| **Biological Parents / Full Siblings** | Solid Line | Default (no `stroke-dasharray`) |
| **Spouses / Partners** | Standard Dash | `stroke-dasharray="8, 4"` |
| **Adoptive Parents** | Dense Fine Dots | `stroke-dasharray="1, 4"` `stroke-linecap="round"` |
| **Step Parents / Step Siblings** | Long Dash, Short Gap | `stroke-dasharray="12, 3, 3, 3"` |
| **Half Siblings** | Alternating Dash-Dot | `stroke-dasharray="10, 4, 2, 4"` |
| **Foster / Guardianship** | Wide Spaced Dots | `stroke-dasharray="2, 8"` `stroke-linecap="round"` |
| **Divorced / Separated** | Slash/Dotted Pattern | Custom dasharray or line offset |

---

### Code Examples

```xml
<!-- Spouses: Classic Dashed Line -->
<line x1="50" y1="50" x2="250" y2="50" 
      stroke="#333" stroke-width="2" 
      stroke-dasharray="8, 4" />

<!-- Adoptive: Smooth Circular Dots -->
<line x1="50" y1="90" x2="250" y2="90" 
      stroke="#0066cc" stroke-width="3" 
      stroke-dasharray="0, 8" stroke-linecap="round" />

<!-- Step Relationship: Dash-Dot Combo -->
<line x1="50" y1="130" x2="250" y2="130" 
      stroke="#666" stroke-width="2" 
      stroke-dasharray="12, 4, 3, 4" />

```

### Pro-Tip for Dotted Lines

To get true, round circular dots instead of small squarish rectangles,
set `stroke-dasharray="0, [gap_size]"` (or `1, [gap_size]`) and add `stroke-
linecap="round"`. The `round` cap expands the 0-length dash into a
perfect circle!

